    idealAnswer: 'ROC plots TPR vs. FPR (False Positive Rate = FP / (FP + TN)). In highly imbalanced datasets (e.g., fraud with 99.9% legitimate negatives and 0.1% fraud), the True Negative count (TN) is massive. Large increases in false positives (e.g., 100 to 5,000) barely shift FPR because the denominator is huge, yielding an overly optimistic ROC-AUC of 0.98+.\n\nPrecision-Recall AUC plots Precision (TP / (TP + FP)) vs. Recall (TP / (TP + FN)). PR-AUC focuses exclusively on the minority positive class and does not include TN. A spike in false positives degrades Precision immediately. For fraud detection, PR-AUC is the strictly correct evaluation metric.',
